                        "review": "Legend of Mercy is a Chinese indie fantasy game that explores themes of, among things, romantic love, conflict, alliances and tensions between nations, and racism.\n\nIt is a mix of a Visual Novel with a romantic gift system that ties into a letter system you can pick up from your mailbox. And a point and click open world with mini-games centered around either treating and healing a patient and on the other hand combat. \n\nThe English translation is good at least on a basic level ( I am not a grammar lover nor do I know Chinese), though the love poems comes off as lackluster some times more than others which are probably due to the hardship in translating more complex writing.\n\nRomance and sexuality\n\nThe fan service on some of the females, like with the armor with the rank and file High Elf soldiers, is jarring and a bit silly, though some of the females and their outfits do look very enticing and sexy.\nFurthermore it is a bit peculiar in the internal juxtaposition with the romance element of the game, which focuses by and large, with deviations, more on eroticism and romance than pure pornography, hedonism and soulless nudity. \nThere's a lot of potential female romance interest in the game, I'd would have preferred a smaller number and then that they would have been given more screen time instead. \n\nThe Plot and the setting  \n\n The main plot is simple but functions overall most of the time, though the main sentient antagonist isn't fully fleshed out which made certain segments rather silly, maybe the developers ran out of time. Also the use of foreshadowing stroke me as peculiar on a couple of occasions.\nThe setting lends itself to explore some important real life issues, like mercy and racism and conflict between nations, though I found it and the plot a bit simplistic and unrealistic and unintentionally comical at times. \nAlso while the way, for instance, High Elves and Dark Elves are used in the setting might be tolerable for a Chinese audience, presuming the don't know anything of Tolkien nor the subsequent western fantasy medium, it would more or less be jarring for a western perspective.     \n",
